Overview

This document is dedicated to explaining how users can assign permissions to records – this applies to any record that supports permissions, such as enquiries, quotes, jobs, structures, etc.

Prerequisites

As a security consideration, only users with administrative privileges can set permissions against records of any kind.

How do Permissions Work?

Whenever a new record of any type is created, they get assigned a default permission type of “Everyone” – this means that anyone can view & made changes to them, even if two users belong to different organisation units. Please, note that the default permission cannot be deleted. Given that there is a record we wish to limit, it can be done so in two ways:

Assign permission on a user level, meaning that only the person with the permission can view/edit the record.

Assign permission on a group level, which means only the group with the permission can view/edit the record

Permission Hierarchy

To implement the permission mechanism for CymBuild, SharePoint was utilised. With SharePoint, permissions can be either assigned to users or groups, the latter which is simply a collection of users. Imagine there is SharePoint folder without any restriction. This would mean that anyone with the link for the folder can view its content and make changes to it.

If a permission was added for – let’s say – group “A” with read & write access, now only those members of group “A” could access the folder

If a secondary permission was applied for a user this time (with read & write access), only members of group “A” and the newly specified user could access the folder.

If we applied a third permission, this time for someone who is a member of group “A”, with denied read access, the person would not be able to access the folder anymore, essentiall'
+ N'y overwriting the original permission.

Assigning Permissions

The process of assigning permission to any record is universal in terms of the steps that need to be performed. To add a permission, look for the Task menu button (located in the top-right corner of the screen) and hover over it to see the options in the dropdown. In this case, the one we need is simply titled “Permissions”.

Figure 1 - Permissions option for the Task menu button

Upon clicking it, a modal will appear with a single grid inside. If no one has set a custom permission yet, it should only have one line-item with the value “Everybody”. This might differ when viewing job as their default permission might be for the organisation unit the job belongs to.

Figure 2 - Default permission of "Everyone" applied to the record.

To add a new permission, click the “New” button above the grid which in turn will open a secondary modal where we can enter the permission. In this modal, we have two separate dropdowns, one for selecting users that exist in CymBuild, while the second dropdown contains values for user groups. Additionally, four checkboxes can be seen under the “Settings” group.

Figure 3 - Secondary modal for defining permissions

The four checkboxes pertain to the read/write access that is to be assigned to either the group or the user, and at least two of them must be selected: one for reading (to allow or deny) and one for writing (again, to either allow or deny).  For example:

If we wish to apply a permission where the user/group can read but cannot write to the record, the option “Can Read” and “Deny Write” must be ticked.

If we wish to completely deny access to a record, both “Deny Write" and “Deny Read” must be ticked.

Applying Permissions for Users

To set a permission against a user, first select the user in question from the dropdown labelled as “User Id”. Next, select the appropriate read-write access by checking the corresponding checkboxes. Save the record and the permissions should be applied.

Applying Permissions for User Groups

To set a permission against a group, similar to how we set them for users, first, select a group from the dropdown labelled as “Group Id” & select the appropriate checkboxes. Finally, save the record.

Removing Permissions

Should there be a need for removing a set permission, it can be achieved easily. Open the applied permission in the grid by double clicking it & then click the “Delete” option.

Double clickDouble click

