Purpose

This document is intended to give a detailed explanation around the process of generating fee proposal documents from the initial enquiry. The document is automatically generated and holds key information pertaining to the project, which is sourced from the previously created quotes – more importantly, this can be done for both single and multi-discipline proposals, meaning that the system automatically combines the necessary information into a single document, while providing flexibility to modify the scope, prices, and deliverables of the project.

Overview

The first step begins after the enquiry from a client has been received by the team(s). An enquiry is created with information pertaining to basic client information – if not already in the system – and the discipline(s) requested by the client.

Next, the created enquiry must be assessed to decide whether a quote can be provided. If it can, the status of the enquiry is converted to “Live”, otherwise the client is notified that SOCOTEC UK is unable to quote for the requested work.

The enquiry has been accepted, now the quote(s) must be prepared: in case of a multi-discipline work, the responsible teams are notified to ensure they are aligned to provide timely updates.  In this stage that they start preparing their respective quotes. These quotes should be linked to a project, which if it does not already exist, it must be created.

The fee proposal should only be sent if they are all marked as “Ready to Send” - otherwise, each team will continue to revise their quotes until they are ready.

If the client accepts the proposal, the quotes are marked as “Accepted” and are finalised, followed by the creation of the necessary jobs.

If the client does not accept the initial proposal, the team must assess if a new, revised quote can be created, followed by an updated proposal sent to the client. Otherwise, the initial enquiry is marked as rejected and is closed.

Generating a Fee Proposal

This section is di'
+ N'vided into two parts: the first part (Preliminary Steps) explains the steps that need to be taken before a fee proposal can be sent, while the second part highlights how and where the fee proposal document can be generated.

Preliminary Steps

Create a new project.

Create a new enquiry through the project you just created. This can be done by locating the “Project Details” grid at the bottom. When the enquiry has loaded, fill out all required fields and add the enquiry services required by the client.

Figure 1 - New Enquiry

Figure 2 - Enquiry Services

Once all required information has been added & everything looks correct, ensure “Is Ready for Quote Review” has been ticked before progressing onto the next step.

Figure 3 - "Is Ready for Quote Review" field

Next step is to create the quote(s) - this can be easily done by navigating to the top of the page and looking for the button labelled as “Tasks”, next to the “Save” and “Save and Exit button. To create the quote(s), simply hover over the button and select “Create Quote(s) from Enquiry”.

Figure 4 - Creating quote(s) from the task menu

Upon successful creation, a green text box should appear at the top of the page. Otherwise, you will see a red one with a message indicating why the quote(s) could not be created.

Figure 5 - Successfully created quote message

Additionally, by going to the “Enquiry Services” grid, we can see that the status of the quote is now changed to “New”.

Once all the quotes have been generated, each team (if multi-discipline) will tweak their respective quote until they get to a stage where they all can be marked as “Ready to Send”.  Once they are, the fee proposal document can be generated.

Figure 6 - All quotes are accepted.

Generating The Fee Proposal

Since any quote that has been created – may that be single or multi-discipline – originate from the initial enquiry, generating the fee proposal can be done through the enquiry itself.

The steps are simple: go to the enquiry in question, and like how the quotes were created, we need to utilise the task button: hover over it and find the option called “Document Tasks”. Clicking this will bring up a modal where we can select a document we would like to have generated - select the one titled “Fee Proposal”. To make the search easier, the “Filter by Name” field can be used.

Once selected, click “Generate Documents” which should bring up the option to have the document generated either as a Word or PDF document.  This will trigger document generation – one copy will be saved to SharePoint while another copy is downloaded locally to your workstation.

Fee Proposal Document

Structure

Since we can have the document generate for both single and multi-discipline projects, their content will slightly differ. To make it easier to visualise, a diagram has been included that details the key sections.

