Purpose

This document will explain not only how the user can generate documents with merge fields in them, but also how to reference these from CymBuild itself.

Overview

CymBuild’s merge document functionality enables the creation of professional documents by automatically sourcing and inserting key values from relevant records (when Document Tasks are enabled). For instance, when handling an enquiry, users can generate a fee proposal that automatically populates data from the enquiry. Additionally, the “Merge Document Items” feature allows users to incorporate images, tables, and other elements, not just text.

Processes

Creating a Merge Document

To create a merge document, the following steps are required:

Assemble the document and identify every location where unique information—such as the client’s name and address, project fees, and descriptions—will be inserted.

Convert these placeholders into merge fields, Content Controls and Bookmarks using Microsoft Word.

Merge Fields

What are Merge Fields?

A merge field in Microsoft Word serves as a placeholder—a “blank space” that is later populated with specific information. In CymBuild, merge fields are used to insert predefined pieces of information associa'
+ N'ted with the records you’re working with (such as enquiries, quotes, jobs, or structures). These fields are uniquely formatted by enclosing a word within double angle brackets. For example:

• «DescriptionOfWorks»• «SignatoryJobTitle»

The figure below, taken from a fee proposal document, illustrates a real-life example of this in action.

Figure 1 - Merge Fields on a document

Adding Merge Fields

Note

Please, ensure to use the desktop version of Microsoft Word as inserting merge fields on the online version is not supported.

There are two ways to add a merge field in a document:

First Approach

Second Approach

Click where you want to place a merge field

Press CTRL+F9, which will place an icon like this: { }

Click where you want to place a merge field

Click “Insert” in the toolbar at the top and click “Quick Parts”. Select the “Field” from the dropdown.

In both cases, you should see a pop-up window as below in the figure.

Figure 2 - Window for adding merge fields to a document.

From here, there are only two additional steps that need to be done:

In the list on the left hand-side, locate the option “MergeField”

Once selected, enter a value for the “Field Name” field. Please, ensure that the value is identical (e.g. case sensitive) to that described in the merge field  table.

Click “OK” and the field should be added to the document.

An example of this can be seen below (Figure 3).

It is important to mention that the user adding these fields should keep track of all field names as later these will need to be referenced in CymBuild.

Figure 3 - Example of a configured merge field.

Editing Merge Fields

Should there be a need to edit a merge field, it can be done quite easily: right-click on the field and select “Edit Field”. This should bring up the window as seen in figure 4.

Figure 4 - Editing an existing merge field.

Content Controls

What are Content Controls?

Content controls act as containers that can contain content such as images, dates, tables, or even formatted text. Content controls were introduced recently, as previously bookmarks were also used for images and data tables – something which turned out to be inefficient & even caused documents to be locked when downloaded from SharePoint. As such, content controls are now used for images, data tables, and signatures.

Adding a Content Control

Note

To place content control elements in a document, we need to have access to the Developer section in Word. For this, right-click in the toolbar at the top and select “Customise the Ribbon”. Tick the option “Developer” and then “Ok”.

Once the Developer section becomes available, look for Rich Text Control - clicking this will add it to where the cursor is on the document.

Figure 5 - Option for adding rich control text

Figure 6 - Content Control

Editing a Content Control

After placing a content control placeholder using the rich text control, assign its properties by clicking the “Properties” option in the toolbar at the top to open a new window. In this window, enter a value in the tag field—this serves as the bookmark or control name in CymBuild. The tag should consist solely of alphabetic text without numbers, spaces, or special characters, and it must be remembered during setup, as all bookmarks, tags, and merge fields in CymBuild are case sensitive. Optionally, you can also enter a title to make it easier to identify the purpose of the content control, especially when there are multiple controls in the document. .

Figure 7 - Setting the properties for the content control

Figure 8 - Content control after setting the properties

Deleting a Content Control

To remove a content control, simply click on it and then press the delete button.

Bookmarks

What are Bookmarks?

In Microsoft Word, bookmarks are like digital placeholders or markers that you can set in your document to easily find specific spots later. '
+ N'They can also be assigned names like merge fields. In CymBuild, they are utilized as placeholders for other documents we wish to include, although currently, this only support the SOCOTEC Terms and Conditions document.  In a document, they can be located by looking for the following icon:

Adding Bookmarks

Note

Bookmarks are not enabled by default in Word. To enable them, please go File > Options > Advanced and enable the option “Show Bookmarks”.

Figure 9 - Enabling bookmarks in Microsoft Word.

To insert a bookmark, click “Insert” at the top and locate the bookmark icon: Once clicked, a pop-up window will appear where you can add, remove, and go to a selected bookmark. To insert one, enter a name at the top input field and then click “Add”. Please, note, that the bookmark will be added where the cursor is located.

Figure 10 - Bookmark window.

Editing Bookmarks

Bookmarks cannot be edited – such as renaming them. If you have added a bookmark which you wish to rename, it must be first deleted and then re-added with the new name.

Delete Bookmarks

To delete a bookmark, click the icon we use to add them. From the list, select the bookmark you wish to delete (the selection should become highlighted) and then simply click the “Delete” button.

Additional Operations for Bookmarks

The following features can also be used with bookmarks – these can come useful when trying to locate/navigate bookmarks:

“Go To” Button: Located in the same window as where we add/delete bookmarks. Clicking the button will take you to the exact location of the bookmark selected in the list.

Sort by: The bookmarks in the list can be sorted either by their name or their location.

Adding Merge Documents to CymBuild

This section details how to add a completed document to CymBuild. We will be covering the following steps:

Placing the document in the correct folder on SharePoint

Creating a reference to the document in CymBuild

Uploading the Document to SharePoint (For use by super users)

As of now, CymBuild provides the option to have a document with merge field generated for five entity types: enquiries, quotes, jobs, accounts, and property. Depending on which one the document is for the user will have to place the document in a folder assigned to the given type.  Below is a table with a link provided for each folder.

