Overview

The updated My Work Grid boasts a refreshed design, enhanced data display, and new color-coding features that set it apart from the previous version. In this document, we''''ll detail all the new enhancements and demonstrate how to utilise the updated functionalities.

New Design

Within CymBuild, users engage with several types of grids daily. For instance, the standard dynamic grids not only display data but also enable the creation of new records when needed. Additionally, batch grids—featuring checkboxes for each line item—are primarily designed for editing multiple records simultaneously.

This new Grid boasts the following:

Multi-Column Dynamic Grid

To meet the new My Work grid''''s requirements, we introduced a grid type called the multi-column dynamic grid. This grid enables a hierarchical organization of categories, with primary columns at the top that can span multiple secondary columns (or sub-columns).

Note

With 29 columns, the grid is notably wide. Although you can maximize the widget size to display as many columns as possible and minimize horizontal scrolling, it is advisable to use full-screen mode for the best user experience.

Main ColumnMain Column

Main Column

Main Column

Sub-ColumnsSub-Columns

Sub-Columns

Sub-Columns

Limitations

When working with these grids, keep the following in mind:

The main category cannot be filtered, although filtering remains available for its sub-columns.

Unlike traditional dynamic grids where the first column can be locked to remain visible during horizontal scrolling, this layout does not support that feature. Instead, you can lock the entire main column, which means all sub-columns within it will remain visible during horizontal scrolling.

New Functionality

Colouring Main Columns

Custom colouring functionality has been added to the main columns (excluding the sub-columns), allowing adjustments without entering edit mode. Additionally, a tooltip has '
+ N'been integrated into the main headers, displaying a message when hovered over.

Figure 1 - Hint message to make the user aware the column can be coloured

Applying a Colour

To assign a colour to a header, simply right-click on the header text. This action will display a drop-down option labelled "Change Colour." Once selected, a colour palette icon will appear next to the header.

Figure 2 - Option for enabling the colour palette.

Figure 3 - Colour palette for applying colour

Once the desired colour has been selected, simply click “Apply” and the colour will be applied to the header. Please, ensure to save the dashboard to prevent losing the applied colouring.

Removing a Colour

To remove a colour, follow the same steps as when applying one – but when opening the colour palette, simply click the “Cancel” button, and the colour will be stripped. Again, please ensure to save the dashboard to ensure the colour will not be applied the next time the widget dashboard is opened.

Remembering Filters

Filters applied to sub-columns are now automatically retained even after closing CymBuild, with no extra steps required from the user.

Note
