Widget in Full-Screen Mode8

Overview

The widget dashboard allows users to add custom components such as gauges and grids in the form of “widgets”, which can be thought of as tiles in a grid with customisable properties like size, order, and colouring. Using the dashboard enables users to have data – tailored to their individual needs – in a centralised location, eliminating the need for jumping between locations across the system to gather information.

The Dashboard

The landing screen for CymBuild is the dashboard and as such will be the first thing to load when a user opens up CymBuild. To access it from the side menu, navigate the “Home” menu item which will be first one starting from the top.

When loading up the dashboard for the first time, it will be empty. In the centre of it, there is a useful message that says, “Start a new dashboard by clicking ‘Edit’”. The edit button can be found in the top-right corner of the screen, which if clicked, enables the functionality to customise the board.

Figure 1 - Empty dashboard

Edit Mode

Edit mode enables the user to not only add or remove widgets but to also customise their states, which can be the size, order, or the colour. In this mode that the user can also save their own configuration to the database. To enable edit mode, the user must click the edit button in the top-right corner. In edit mode, three sets of buttons will become visible at the very top of the page.

Figure 2 - Edit mode buttons

Starting from the left, we have two dropdowns for adding either gauges or grids (figure 3). In the middle, the buttons are used to either increase or decrease the size of all widgets currently on the board (figure 4). On the right (figure 5), we have the save button which is used to save the current state of the dashboard to the database, while the cancel button exits edit mode but does not save any change made to the board.

Figure '
+ N'3 - Buttons for adding widgets

Figure 4 - Buttons for setting size for all widgets on the board

Figure 5 - Save & Cancel buttons

Adding a Widget

Currently, there are only two types of widgets the dashboard supports: the first is grid which we can find throughout CymBuild, while the second one is gauges, which are visual display of data.

Figure 6 Gauge

Figure 7 - Grids

To add a grid, click the dropdown of the desired type and select an option. This will automatically add the widget to the board. This can be repeated until edit mode is disabled. Once a widget has been added, the drop-down will reflect this by disabling the selection for it. If the user has finished adding widgets to the board, they need to make sure to click the save button.

Deleting a Widget

Like when adding widgets, deleting can only be done in edit mode. For this, look for a red bin icon located on the header of the widget. Once clicked, a confirmation message will appear. Click “Ok” to delete, “Cancel” otherwise.

Delete buttonDelete button

Delete button

Delete button

Figure 8 - Delete button for a widget

Figure 9 - Confirmation message shown when attempting to delete a widget.

Reordering

Given there are more than one active widgets on the board, the user can change the order in which they are displayed by dragging them by their header. Upon hovering over the header of the grid, the mouse icon should become of something resembling a cross with an arrow on each end . Simply hold down the left mouse button and drag the widget to the desired location.

Manual Resizing

The width and height of an individual widget can be set. For this, align the mouse icon with the border of the widget – the icon should change a line with an arrow on each end . As the border is being dragged, the user can see the widget take on the new size in real time.

Colouring Widgets

The header of a widget can be set to a custom colour to help the user colour code key widgets. For this, look for the colour palette icon that is located to the right of the delete icon. When clicked, a series of colours will be displayed from which the user can select. One a colour is picked, click “Apply” to apply the colour to the header. When finished, ensure to save the board.

Figure 10 - Applying colour to a widget

Miscellaneous Functionality

Widget in Full-Screen Mode

To make reading grids with large amount of data easier, a full-screen mode has been added to the widgets. When enabled, the grid will take up the whole screen. For gauges, this will open the associated grid.

To toggle full screen for a widget, look for the diagonal expand icon .

Figure 11 - Widget in full-screen mode

