Overview

This document is dedicated to the new status management functionality in CymBuild which is simply referred to as workflows, which is utilised by enquiries, quotes, and jobs. Previously, statuses (e.g. “Ready for Quote”, “Sent”, “Cancelled”, etc.)  were managed using the fields found on each record type. Although functional, it lacked the ability to add comments for things like:

Anything that is required from the business/client.

Any supporting evidence is required.

Workflows

Workflows essentially encompass a set of sequential statuses that can be applied to a given record to track its progress and allow transitioning it through the different stages of a job.

Default Workflows

System Default workflows are predefined workflows modelled on how statuses were historically set prior to the introduction of workflows. They exist within the system as fallback options, ensuring continuity and consistency, especially when creating new workflows, as they will be applied by default, which the business units can build on top of – that is, if there is no active custom workflow  to overwrite it, but more on that later. These workflows are fixed and cannot be modified, allowing for a standardized set of universal statuses across CymBuild.

Custom Workflows

Although, default workflows are non-editable by nature, there was still a need to allow the different business units to create their own workflows that best fit certain record types with their own custom statuses and progression logic. As mentioned in the Default Workflows section, custom workflows implement default workflows which the users can build on, meaning that certain statuses will always be present. For example, quotes will have “Ready to Send”, “Declined”, “Accepted”, etc.

Workflow Transitions

Workflow transitions are configurations used by a workflow to define the order of statuses that can be set against a record type from start to finish. They define '
+ N'the subsequential logic for workflow status – in essence, they describe what status(es) are valid choices based on the currently set status. For example, we can mark a quote as “Sent”, meaning we are waiting for feedback from the client in terms of approval – therefore, we can set transitions up that allow setting either “Accepted” or “Declined” as valid options from “Sent”.

Figure 1 - Adding a transition to the workflow

Field

Description

Enabled

Marks if the transition for the workflow is active. If not enabled, the transition will not be selectable on the record type.

Description

Option description of the transition.

From Status

Denotes the previous transition after which this transition is shown. For example, if the previous transition is “Waiting for customer approval”, we might only want the option “Accepted” or “Declined”.

To Status

As explained for “From Status” above. To Status denotes the status that should be shown after the value set for From Status

Sort Order

The order in which it should be shown In the “Workflow Transitions” grid. Purely cosmetic, does not affect functionality.

Is Final

Marks if the transition is the last available status, meaning the record will be locked down and marked as “Complete”.

Creating a New Workflow

Figure 2- Adding a new workflow

To create a custom workflow, the only required pieces of information are the entity type (e.g. enquiry, quote, or job) it is for and a unique name. Additionally, it is a good idea to specify the organisational unit which is the workflow is for.  Once the workflow has been created, the default workflow transitions are automatically populated in the “Workflow Transitions” grid.

Figure 3 - Newly created custom workflow

Additionally, we can see a new field appear, called “Enabled” which controls whether the workflow is enabled for the specified organisation unit & entity type. Once ticked, the workflow will overwrite the default when working with the record type. Since there can only be one active custom workflow per organization unit & entity type, if there is any other active one, upon clicking the enable checkbox, a validation message will be shown – in this case, the active workflow must be disabled.

Figure 4 - Validation message shown when trying to enable a custom workflow while there is already an active one.

To add a new transition on top of the existing one, simply click the new button for the “Workflow Transitions” grid.

Workflow Statuses

A list of system wide available statuses can be found in Settings after opening an arbitrary workflow configuration & navigating to the “Workflow Statuses” tab. In the grid, which statuses can be selected for the different entity types, their description, and whether they are enabled. In the top-right corner, we have a new button that allows users to add custom statuses that fit their need.

Custom Statuses

Clicking the new button will bring up a modal where we can enter the necessary information required for the workflow status. Below is a table of all the options we can set:

Figure 5 - Screen for setting up a custom workflow.

General Fields

Field

Description

Enabled

Marks if the status is enabled. If enabled, it can be added to a transition as long as it is for the right entity type (entity type can be set under the “Show In Record” section.

Name

The name of the status – for example, “Waiting for Customer”, “Sent”, “Declined”, etc.

Description

Description for the status – optional.

Organisational Unit

The organisation unit that can use this status. Other organisational units will not be able to select it when setting up their workflows.

Sort Order

Used to define the position in the “Workflow Statuses”. Purely a cosmetic property, it has no bearing over functionality.

Requires User Action

If enabled, the record will display a “Requires User Action” data pill at the top of the page. Can be '
+ N'used to emphasise that a record requires additional actioning.

Is Predefined

This field is disabled on custom workflows – used by the default workflows for distinguishing purposes – as such, it can be ignored.

Show In Record Fields

These fields simply denote what record types can utilise the workflow.

Field

Description

Show In Enquiries

If enabled, the status will become available for enquiries (as long the organisation unit of the record is the same)

Show In Quotes

If enabled, the status will become available for quotes.

Show In Jobs

If enabled, the status will become available for jobs.

Status Settings Fields

Field

Description

Is Active Status

Marks if the record is active.

Is Customer Waiting Status

Used to mark a record with a data pill called “Customer Waiting”.

Is Complete Status

If enabled, the status is treated as the last, finalising status, after which the record is locked & considered complete.

UI Fields

Currently, these fields are not yet utilized, but their main purpose for the future is to enable the user to colour code & set descriptive icons for statuses.

Field

Description

Colour

The text colour of the status (e.g. red, yellow, etc.)

Icon

A predefined list of icons which can be set against a status.

Notifications Fields

Used to take advantage of the recently implemented functionality by which we can enforce authorisation when a given status is added to a record.

Field

Description

Authorisation Required

If enabled, upon adding a status to a record, authorisation will be required by someone with the correct authorisation. For example, a manager might have to add an additional status to the record (approved/declined)

Send Notification

Used in conjunction with “Authorisation Required”. When adding a status this setting enabled, people with the right authorisation in a given organisation unit will be notified by email.'', IsAuthoritative = 1, IsPublished = 1, PublishedUtc = ''2026-06-03 15:10:59.6910965'', CreatedByUserId = 722, UpdatedByUserId = 722, CreatedUtc = ''2026-06-03 15:10:59.6910965'', UpdatedUtc = ''2026-06-03 15:52:07.2361005'' WHERE ID = 27 AND RowStatus = 1 AND Title = N''TM - Workflow'' AND Slug = N''sharepoint-drives-b-rn0a3cmntewrc6jxnw4s987d1vqr2-reudnhtp5j5cbtgd2quthut4s9ge1vckbz-root-general-training-material-business-process-documentations-tm-workflow-tm-workflow-1'' AND KnowledgeCategoryId IS NULL
