Introduction

Outlook Email Filer is an add-in created to work specifically with CymBuild. Its purpose is to allow the user to file an email – which will be available on SharePoint - against various records we have in the system, such as a contact, structure, an invoice, or even jobs.

Purpose

This document is intended to support the user in operating CymBuild Outlook, which is an add-on created to work in conjunction with CymBuild for the purpose of filing emails directly from Outlook against the various projects and records we have in the system.

The structure of the document is as follows: First, we will cover how to access the add-on directly from Outlook as well as providing suggestions if there are problems accessing it. Next, we will discuss the layout, specifically what the different sections do, and what the different buttons do.

Lastly, we will go through the operations available, such as filing emails against records.

Accessing CymBuild Outlook

Requirements

To be able to access the add-in, the user must be part of the correct group, specifically the “CymBuild Outlook” group - this is managed by IT.

Once part of the group, there should be no need for installation, as the add-in will be available out of the box.

How to Find CymBuild Outlook?

To access the add-in is quite straightforward: open an email that needs to be filed, which then should open in its own section on the right hand-side. Look for the section with the Reply/Reply All options – you should find a blue icon resembling a window frame here (the icon is called Apps). Fo'
+ N'r easier reference, please find a screenshot below with the icon circled in red.

Figure 1 - Apps icon

Figure 2 - Finding CymBuild Outlook

Once the icon is clicked, a smaller window should appear with various applications to pick from - look for “CymBuild Outlook”. You should also be able to use the search bar in this window to search for it should you not see it.  Once found, simply click it & the add-on should open on the right hand-side of the screen. For reference, you should see an interface as shown in Figure 3.

Figure 3 - Opening the add-on.

I get “Unable to read email and attachment data”. What should I do?

Figure 4 - "Unable to read email and attachment data" error.

If you get the above error – as shown in figure 4 -, the add-in is trying to sign you in into your SOCOTEC account.  Look for a window as shown in the figure below & click your account to sign in with.

Figure 5 - Selecting your account.

What If the Add-on is not Showing?

If you cannot find the add-on in the apps, it means that you have not been added to the “CymBuild Outlook” group - Please, check with IT as they are the one in charge of this.

Please, note that all new users to CymBuild should assigned to this group by default, therefore this issue should not arise.

Limitations/Considerations

Filing multiple emails against one or more records at the same time

Currently – due to limitations enforced by Microsoft -, the user cannot file more than one email at the time.

For example, multiselecting emails & then trying to file all of them at once will not work. Instead, only the email that is opened at the time will file.

Measures have been added to better the user experience.

The Interface

Introduction

In this section, we will go over the user interface in detail, specifically the different sections there are & their core functionality.

Overview

Once CymBuild Outlook is opened, you should see the following window. The interface for the add-on is simple and can be described as single paged as everything – in terms of functionality – can be found within one page. Altogether, we have four different sections:

Add-in header.

Section containing information regarding the currently viewed email.

Search area.

Information regarding the add-in.

Sections

Going from top to bottom, let us discuss the various sections such as their general purpose & what the different buttons do.

Add-on Header

The header simply includes the name of the add-in, a button for pinning the add-on to the side when switching between emails (otherwise, the add-on will close) , and a button for closing the add-on.

Quick Actions

The quick actions streamline user interaction by providing shortcuts to essential actions & reducing the need to navigate through multiple steps.

(File with Previous)

Takes the record that was last used to file an email against & files the currently selected email against the same record.

(Refresh)

It simply refreshes the search result.

(Save To SharePoint)

When clicked, the email will be filed against the selected record(s).

Note

Unlike the “File with Previous” button, selection must be made prior to clicking the button.

Email Information

Just above the search result section, we have a section for displaying information pertaining to the selected email. Below is a breakdown of what the different buttons do.

(Search Results)

Takes the user to the search results section.

(Attachments)

Shows the attachments for the selected email. This includes:

Preview: Type of file, such as image, docx, PDF, etc.

Name: Name of the file.

Inline: Embedded in the email or attached

Immutable: TODO

(Email Information)

Shows general email information, such as who the sender is, the subject, email ID, etc.

(Filing Settings)

Settings for exporting emails. Currently, we have the following two settings:

Move Filed Email To "CymBuild File'
+ N'd": Determines if the email should be moved to the “CymBuild Filed” folder after filing the email.

Extract Attachments To Separate Folder: If ticked, everything attached to the email will be placed into a separate folder on SharePoint – Otherwise, both the email & attachments will share the same folder.

(Open Helper Documentation)

Opens a brief helper documentation that gives a quick rundown on the what the various buttons do.

Search Results

In the middle, we can find the search section where the user can search for the different record types available in CymBuild. Within this section, we have the search bar at the top , followed by a default tag (Type) which is utilized to group the search results by record types available in the system and can be removed if needed by clicking the “X” inside the tag.

By looking at figure x and y, we can see how using the “Type” tag makes the search result display. Each tag can collapse or expand if needed. If the tag is not being used, the result is displayed in one continuous list.

Hovering a search result

When hovering over a given search result, additional information is displayed pertaining to the record, such as the name, the record type, as well as the ID. This works in both cases

Selecting a Search Result

When there is a result that we would like to file against, we can simply click it – this will result in the result being appended in light blue just above the search bar. We can add multiple of them if needed.

Next to each appended record, we will find a red bin icon which we can use to unselect an item.

Save to SharePoint Button

Lastly, for this section we have the button for filing email. Like the button described in the Quick Actions section, a record must be selected prior to trying to file an email.

Once clicked, the email will have a category appended to it which will be visible in the subject section of the email. Altogether, there are three distinct categories that can be appended.

Shows just after clicking the save button – this will change to either “Filed” or “Filed Error” depending on if it was successful.

The email was filed successfully.

The email could not be filed due to an error.

Add-in Information

At the very bottom, we can find information pertaining to the add-in, which includes the current version number, copyright details, etc.

Operations

