Enquiry

Fill in all compulsory fields marked in red.

Hit the Save button – this ensures that the record is created.

Before an enquiry can become a quote, it must be reviewed first – tick the “Is Ready for Quote Review” checkbox.

Please, note, that this may produce new compulsory fields.

In the “Enquiry Details” section, add the job type required à Save.

From the “Tasks” button, select “Create Quote(s) from Enquiry”.

Enquiry Details

Figure 59 – Enquiry Section

The following areas are available under Enquiry Details:

Enquiry Services:

The service(s) required at this stage.

Enquiry Key Dates:

The user can add key dates if needed.

Figure 60 - Enquiry Details

NOTE:

Once a quote has been created from an enquiry, the quote number can be found on the enquiry record it originates from. To do this, open the enquiry and scroll down to the “Enquiry Details” grid found at the bottom of the page.

Figure 61- Finding the quote number on an enquiry record.

Quote

Begin by creating a new quote section under “Quote Details”.

Fill out all the compulsory fields à click Save.

Once saved, the “Quote Details” should become available to edit.

Add necessary item(s) to the section.

Once the quote is ready, tick “Is Final (Ready to Send)” à Save.

All fields outside the quote status panel should now become read-only and cannot be edited.

Quote Section:

The following areas are available under Quote Section:

Sections:

List of sections containing information on the job requirement and any relevant product items. (See the information section with Figure 64 & 65 below)

Fee Drawdown:

Shows the total values for each RIBA stage of the quote based on sections above.

Job Creation Summary:

Shows all the Jobs that are to be created once quote has been finalised. This will aid the user in understanding if Sections are consolidated before generating the jobs. (Figure 66)

Created Jobs:

Links to the job(s) created from the quote.

Quote Memos:

The user can add a memo to the record.

Quote Key Dates:

The user can add key dates with details.

Figure 62- Quote Page

Figure 63- Quote Details Grid.

INFORMATION

Before converting a quote into a job, it is important to mention the functionality to consolidate jobs that are entered into the Quote Details section. In the “Quote Details” section, under the drawer “Section”, the user can add these jobs, but it is important to acknowledge that by producing a quote will create the same number of jobs as the number of items present in the list (Figure 64).

Figure 64- Quote Details Section.

To tac'
+ N'kle this, open each task from the list and tick the ‘Consolidate Jobs’. This will create only one job record type which will hold all the jobs that are marked as Consolidate (Figure 65).

Figure 65- Consolidate Jobs.

You can confirm the jobs are created by viewing the Job Creation Summary (Figure 66)

Figure 66 - Job creation summary.

NOTE:

If the need arises to edit a quote after ticking the ‘is final’ field, simply untick the field and all other fields should become editable again. Exception to this is when a quote has already generated the required jobs, in which case the enquiry cannot be edited anymore.

Job

To create a job, we must first  generate from an accepted quote.

Locate the desired quote.

Click the “Tasks” button and select “Create Jobs”.

Upon successful creation, you will find the job under the “Jobs” section.

Once open, fill all the required fields, and then save the record.

Figure 67 - Job Page

Job Tabs

Figure 68 - Job Tabs

NOTE:

When working with transaction related data, updating the figures in the “Job Transaction” section will be reflected in the “Job Fees” section.

Earlier in this document, the tabs found in the job section were briefly touched on, although the exact functionalities were not discussed.

Job:

Holds information pertaining to the job itself. This includes the description, job status, both client & agent details, etc.(Figure 69)

Fees:

Holds payment/transaction information, broken down into three categories:

(Figure 70)

Project Details:

People involved in the job (and their contact information) can be found and added here – for example, architects, structural engineers, designers, etc. Additionally, we also have the “Project Key Dates” table as well.(Figure 71)

Milestones:

Milestones of the project can be managed here – adding new ones as well as excluding existing ones, marking them as completed. Both Activities and Actions can also be added from this tab. These will be reflected on the “Job” tab at the top.(Figure 72)

Activities:

Job related activities that need to be carried & are integral part of the process. This can include sending emails, meetings, inspections, etc.(Figure 73)

Actions:

Unlike activities, actions can be added that are not necessarily part of the job process that require further action. For example, during an inspection, the fire door is discovered not to be shutting properly and must be fixed.(Figure 74)

Figure 69- Job.

Figure 70- Fees.

Figure 71- Project Details.

Figure 72- Milestones.

Figure 73 - Activities.

Figure 74 - Actions.

