Basic Operations

In this section of the document, the user is guided through the different processes they can complete, starting from creating an enquiry to all the way to working with settings. This includes the necessary steps that must be undertaken to be able to create that given process.

Parent-Child Relationship

When working with different record types, certain fields can affect the behaviour of others: we refer to this as having parent-child relationship between them. For example, selecting one field might automatically assign options to another field, or even make other fields disappear.  Let us use the “Client Details” section from an enquiry to illustrate this. As we can see in the figure below (Figure 31), we have selected a client in the first field, but all the other fields are left blank.

Figure 31- Client Details.

By clicking the dropdown for the “Client Address” field, we can also see there are various addresses associated with the selected client (Figure 32) - this means that once the client field has been selected, only the relevant options are loaded into the dropdowns

Figure 32- Associated client addresses.

After selecting one of the addresses, we can now also see that all the other fields – except for the “Contact” field – have disappeared.

Home Page (Wiget Board)

As of November 2024, a new addition of CymBuild is the widget board which replaces the original home page. A widget board is a customisable area where the user can select from graphical components – called widgets – that show specific type of information. These can be moved around and resized to create a layout that best suits the user’s needs.

Figure 33- Widget Board

Widget Types

Currently, there are two different widget types CymBuild supports, which are grids and gauges. Each widget will have a header that shows its name (e.g. in Figure 35, the name is “Open Quotes not Issued”) and a expand button. Additional functionality becomes available in edit mode.

Gauges

Figure 34 - Gauges

Gauges are various "meters", displaying progress related to processes like jobs, invoices, and appointments and are linked to various grids found across CymBuild. Their purpose is to show data in a concise way.

Grids

Figure 36 - Grid as widget

Comparatively speaking, grids as widgets look and function the exact same as anywhere else you would find a grid in CymBuild. Only distinction – at least in terms of functionality – is the lack of the “New” button that allows creating a new record through a grid.

Additionally, we also have two grids that we can only access from the widget area:

Recent:

Its purpose is to help users keep track of previously viewed records, acting like a history. For instance, Figure 37 shows that the user previously viewed two job records, one with the ID 8509 and another with 8499.

Figure 37 - Recent Items Grid Widget

Scheduled:

This keeps track of any upcoming/ongoing action that is linked to a particular job. As such, it is only displayed when a particular action type is selected.

Figure 38 - Scheduled Items Widget

NOTE:

Scheduler is linked to activity types. You can double check if an activity is going to trigger the scheduler by going to Settings > Activity Types. Check if the “Is Schedule Item” is ticked for that activity.

Functionality

Edit

When navigating to the widget area, the only button available is the “Edit” button which acts as a controller for enabling the customisation functionality of the board. While this button is visible, everything remains static: widgets cannot be added, resized, or even reorganized. Once clicked, a number of buttons will appear which'
+ N' are detail below.

Figure 39 - Edit Button

To disable edit mode, simply click the “Cancel” button. Please, note that any changes made to the board prior to clicking the cancel button will be lost, and the board will default to the previously used layout. This includes the loss of any newly added widgets.

Figure 40 - Button for cancelling edit mode.

Expand

In the upper right corner of the widgets – when not in edit mode – we can find the expand icon. This essentially does two things, depending on which widget is being used with:

As mentioned before, gauges are linked to grids and show the information one would find in the grid in a concise, graphical way. Clicking the expand icon for a gauge will show the original grid in full view.

When used with grids, clicking the expand icon will fit the grid to the whole screen.

Figure 41 - Expand Icon

Adding/Removing Widgets

Once in edit mode, widgets can be added by using the two dropdown lists found on the left side of the screen.

Figure 42 - Dropdown lists for adding widgets.

These will show a list of available widgets that can be added to the board. Clicking an item will automatically add the widget to the bottom of the board. Once added, the item in the list will become greyed out, indicating that the widget is already being used and cannot be re-added.

Figure 43 - Selection from the dropdown.

To remove a widget, look for the red trash bin icon (Figure 44) in the upper right corner of the widget.  Clicking this will bring up a confirmation window asking the user if they are sure they would like to delete the widget (Figure 45).

Figure 44 - Button for removing a widget.

Figure 45 - Confirmation window shown when removing a widget.

Changing Widget Size

Although, the size for each widget can be changed manually, there is functionality to uniformly increase or decrease the size of all widgets present on the user’s board: clicking the + icon will increase the size while clicking the - icon will decrease it. Important to note this will only set the size horizontally. Once the desired width has been set, ensure that the layout is saved by clicking the “Save” button.

Figure 46 - Increase/Decrease size for all widgets.

NOTE:

Both a minimum and a maximum size is set for both widget types when using this functionality to ensure that components do not shrink or increase to an unnecessary level. This behaviour is not present when manually setting the size of a widget. More on this here.

