Data Components

The following components discussed in this section mainly belong to the record type “Job”, although things like data pills can be found when viewing other record types.

Data Pills

Figure 13

Data pills can be found at the top of the page and are used to notify the user of important information related to the currently viewed entity.  In terms of which data pills are shown is entirely dependent on the circumstances surrounding said entity. For example:

A quote has been accepted or rejected:

A job has been cancelled:

An invoice is overdue:

In terms of colour coding, we have four different ones, each denoting a different intent:

Red

Error

Amber

Warning

Green

Success

Blue

Information

Job Progress

Figure 14

The progress bar is specific to the entity type “Job” and is meant to represent the different milestones which have been associated to said project. These are automatically generated when the job is created, although it is important to mention that not every milestone will be necessarily needed.

NOTE:

Milestones can be turned off under the “Milestones” tab by double clicking the desired milestone(s) in the list and selecting the “Is Not Applicable” checkbox in the modal..

Tabs

Figure 15 – Tabs.

Due to all the intricate data that make a job up, tabs have been added for ease of access. Although, these details can be found in their corresponding sections through the navigation bar, this way the user does not have to jump between the different pages.

Once a given tab has been clicked, the grid view should update to reflect the needed information by the user.

Input Components

Special Fields

Below, a group of components are discussed that have specific functionality & behaviour and as such can affect the record type the user is working on.

Compulsory Fields

When creating certain record types, there will be mandatory fields that need to be provided before the record itself can be saved. These will be marked appropriately with a red piece of text underneath (“The value is compulsory.”)

Figure 16- Compulsory Field

Read-Only Fields

Read-only fields – as the name suggests – are fields that we can read their values of but cannot edit them. These fields are grey in colour.

Figure 17 - Read-only field.

NOTE:

Please, note, that when working with enquiries, quotes, and jobs, fields and even the whole record can be triggered to become read-'
+ N'only by manipulating fields. An example of this is discussed here.

Process Specific Fields

In certain cases, some fields will have bearing on processes that surround the record type. An example of this can be found on the record type quote – a field called “Date sent to client” can affect reporting if left unfilled.

Figure 18- A process specific field.

Textboxes

Textboxes are simple components and some like the one below that allow multiline text (e.g. more than one line of text) to be entered. In figure 20, we can see that the colour of the box is grey – this means that the field is essentially disabled, therefore no text can be entered.

Figure 19 – Enabled textbox. Text can be added.

Figure 20 – Disabled textbox. Text cannot be added.

NOTE:

In the bottom right corner of a textbox, a small icon can be found that is used to expand the size of the it.

Dropdown

Dropdowns allow to select a single item from a list of options. This behaviour is different to combo boxes, which allow the selection of more than one item at the time.

Figure 21- Dropdown Field.

Cascading Dropdown

In a cascading dropdown scenario, the options available in one dropdown menu are dependent on the selection made in another dropdown menu.

Example:

For example, assume there are two dropdowns: one can be used to select the client, while the second one is used to select an associated address to the client. By using a cascading dropdown, we can ensure that upon selecting a client, the second dropdown will only load those registered against the selected client.

Combo Box

Like traditional dropdowns, combo boxes simply allow the selection of multiple items from a list, instead of just one item.

Checkbox

When the box is "ticked" or checked, it typically indicates that a particular option or condition is selected or true.

Figure 22- Checkbox example.

Date & Date/Time

Altogether, there are two different components that can be used to input date/time related data:

Date: Allows to input the date only.

Date & Time: Both the date & time are defined.

Note, that minutes can only be selected in the intervals of 15.

NOTE:

To ease using the Minutes selection, you can only select in intervals of 15.

Figure 23 – Date Only

Figure 24 – Date & Time

Number Box

By default, there are two distinct types of inputs for when working with numbers:

One accepts whole numbers/integers, such as when entering the quantity of something.

The second one accepts decimal, used for entering the price on an invoice, for example.

