Buttons

There are various button types that can be found across the application. Below, each one of them are discussed.

Figure 27 - Action buttons.

The first types of buttons being discussed are the one found when working with different record types. Three action buttons:

Tasks

Acts as dropdown for more options. Depending on privileges and the type of record the user is working on that the options in the dropdown might change, meaning it is process driven (e.g. create quote, job, or even Export tasks).

The following are functionalities that remain across the different entity types:

Figure 28 - Tasks Button Options

View Document: Redirects the user to the SharePoint folder that is associated with the current entity.

Record History: A grid view of all the changes that took place while working on the record. It includes the date & time of the change, who made the change, and which field was changed. Double clicking a row will also show you the old and new value.

Permissions: Shows a grid view of the group(s) that can access the record & what privileges they have exactly (e.g. read, write, etc.)

Document tasks: Opens a modal where the user can search for available documents which then can be generated for that record.

Save

Saves the record while remaining on the page.

Save and Exit

Saves the record and returns to the previous page/viewed section.

.

The next two sets of buttons can be found next to input fields.

Figure 29 & 30 can sometimes be seen next to each other along dropdown fields. Figure 29 can be used when there is a need for creating a new record type (e.g. address, project, etc.).  Upon clicking it, the user will have a modal appear where they can enter all the necessary details to create said record type.

Figure 29

Figure 30

Figure 31

Figure 30 can only be used to view data pertaining to a given field – this means that it offers no functionality to create anything but to view data in read-only mode, meaning that even editing existing data is not an option, either.

As for figure 31, in the Quick Access section of the document, we discussed the different gauges the user can interact with. As mentioned, they offer the functionality whereby clicking the eye icon will take the user to the appropriate '
+ N'section. For example, if a gauge is titled “Quotes Ready to Send”, upon clicking the icon, the user will be redirected to a page that contains a list of quotes that are available to send for quote.

Back to Top

In the bottom right corner, the user can find the Back to Top button. Its functionality is simple: if the user has scrolled all the way to the bottom of the page (or even halfway), by clicking it, the page will automatically scroll back to the top.

Figure 32- Back to Top Button.

