Basic Operations

In this section of the document, the user is guided through the different processes they can complete, starting from creating an enquiry to all the way to working with settings. This includes the necessary steps that must be undertaken to be able to create that given process.

Parent-Child Relationship

When working with different record types, certain fields can affect the behaviour of others: we refer to this as having parent-child relationship between them. For example, selecting one field might automatically assign options to another field, or even make other fields disappear.  Let us use the “Client Details” section from an enquiry to illustrate this. As we can see in the figure below (Figure 33), we have selected a client in the first field, but all the other fields are left blank.

Figure 33- Client Details.

By clicking the dropdown for the “Client Address” field, we can also see there are two addresses associated with the selected client (Figure 34). Just by this, we can already see that by selecting a client, associated addresses in the second field are automatically loaded for selection.

Figure 34- Associated client addresses.

After selecting one of the addresses, we can now also see that all the other fields – except for the “Contact” field – have disappeared.

Figure 35- After selecting an address.

Customer Relationship Management (CRM)

Provides a list of customers with their contact details. Altogether, there are four available tabs:

Account:

Main customer related information can be found here, such as name, account status, address, etc. (Figure 36)

Further down, we also have:

Account Addresses: Addresses can be stored against the customer. (Figure 37)

Account Contacts: Storing contact information against a person or a group of people, such as name, position, email, etc. (Figure 38)

Account Memos: Memos can be added for an account.(Figure 39)

Finance:

Finance related information can be stored, such as previous transactions, as well as the turnover.(Figure 40)

Finance related memos can also be added here.(Figure 41)

Contracts:

Contracts associated with the account in question.(Figure 42)

History:

Associated enquiries, quotes, and jobs to the account.(Figure 43)

Figure 36- Account Page.

Figure 37- Account Addresses.

Figure 38 - Account Contacts.

Figure 39 - Account Memos.

Figure 40 - Account Transactions.

Figure 41- Account Finance Memo.

Figure 42- Account Contracts.

Figure 43 - History.

Structure

Shows a list of structures/sites. Upon opening an address, a new page is opened (Figure 44) with all relevant information pertaining to that address, as well as a map showing the exact location of said site if the postcode has been entered and is valid.

Additionally, the user can also see references under the “History” tab (Figure 45) where the address is included – for example, quotes, jobs, and enquiries where the site was present.

Figure 44- Structures Page.

Figure 45 - Structures History.

Enquiry

Fill in all compulsory fields marked in red.

Hit the Save button – this ensures that the record is created.

Before an enquiry can become a quote, it must be reviewed first – tick the “Is Ready for Quote Review” checkbox.

Please, note, that this may produce new compulsory fields.

In the “Enquiry Details” section, add the job type required  Save.

From the “Tasks” button, select “Create'
+ N' Quote(s) from Enquiry”.

Enquiry Details

Figure 46 – Enquiry Section

The following areas are available under Enquiry Details:

Enquiry Services:

The service(s) required at this stage.

Enquiry Key Dates:

The user can add key dates if needed.

Figure 47 - Enquiry Details

NOTE:

Once a quote has been created from an enquiry, the quote number can be found on the enquiry record it originates from. To do this, open the enquiry and scroll down to the “Enquiry Details” grid found at the bottom of the page.

Figure 48- Finding the quote number on an enquiry record.

Quote

Begin by creating a new quote section under “Quote Details”.

Fill out all the compulsory fields  click Save.

Once saved, the “Quote Details” should become available to edit.

Add necessary item(s) to the section.

Once the quote is ready, tick “Is Final (Ready to Send)”  Save.

All fields outside the quote status panel should now become read-only and cannot be edited.

Quote Section:

The following areas are available under Quote Section:

Sections:

List of sections containing information on the job requirement and any relevant product items. (See the information section with Figure 51 & 52 below)

Fee Drawdown:

Shows the total values for each RIBA stage of the quote based on sections above.

Job Creation Summary:

Shows all the Jobs that are to be created once quote has been finalised. This will aid the user in understanding if Sections are consolidated before generating the jobs. (Figure 53)

Created Jobs:

Links to the job(s) created from the quote.

Quote Memos:

The user can add a memo to the record.

Quote Key Dates:

The user can add key dates with details.

Figure 49- Quote Page

Figure 50- Quote Details Grid.

INFORMATION

Before converting a quote into a job, it is important to mention the functionality to consolidate jobs that are entered into the Quote Details section. In the “Quote Details” section, under the drawer “Section”, the user can add these jobs, but it is important to acknowledge that by producing a quote will create the same amount of jobs as the number of items present in the list (Figure 51).

Figure 51- Quote Details Section.

To tackle this, open each task from the list and tick the ‘Consolidate Jobs’. This will create only one job record type which will hold all the jobs that are marked as Consolidate (Figure 52).

Figure 52- Consolidate Jobs.

You can confirm the jobs are created by viewing the Job Creation Summary (Figure 53)

Figure 53 - Job creation summary.

