Overview

This document is intended to go through the different changes that have been applied to CymBuild – this mainly pertains to how the different entity types of functions.

Changes Made

Enquiries Grid

Figure  - Enquiries Grid

There have been a few minor changes made to the enquiry grid – specifically, two columns have been added:

Discipline:

Displays the enquiry services. If only a singe service is associated with the enquiry, it will display the name of the service itself (e.g. CDM, CDM PD, etc.). If it has multiple services, this will show as “Multi Discipline”

Status:

A field calculated from the quote that make up the enquiry.  Can be of five values (New, Ready to Quote, Quoting, Accepted, Complete)

Lead Organisational Unit:

A field that is yet to be added to enquiries and quotes. Since these two types can be viewed by all organisational units, this field will serve as filter to limit the results to those that belong to a given organisational unit.

Enquiry

There have been several changes made to the enquiry page, may that be introducing new fields, tabs, or changing the way existing fields works.

Fields That Have Been Moved from Quotes

There have been fields that used to be found on the quote. These now have been moved to the enquiry since documents are sent out from the enquiry itself.  These fields are the following:

Acceptance fields such as Date Sent to Client, Date of Client Acceptance, etc.

Description of Work

Chase Date

Send Info to Client'
+ N'

Send Info to Agent

New Fields Added

Structure (UPRN) Details

Changes have also been made to the “Structure (UPRN) Details” section to enhance the user interface. A checkbox labelled “Enter New Structure Details” has been introduced to streamline the process.

If the property already exists in the system, it can simply be selected from the dropdown below the checkbox.

If the property is not in the system, ticking the checkbox will display several fields that allow the user to add a new property. Once the property has been added, these fields will disappear, leaving only the checkbox and dropdown (now updated with the newly added property selected).

It’s important to note that these details are captured at the point of Quote Creation, ensuring the necessary property information is logged early in the process.

Figure  - Structure Details without the checkbox clicked.

Figure  - Structure Details with checkbox clicked.

Client Details & Agent Details

The same functionality applies to the “Client Details” and “Agent Details” sections. Selecting the corresponding checkbox will enable you to enter the required information for each.

Additionally, the “Client Details” section includes an extra checkbox labelled “Use Client as Finance Account.” If this checkbox is selected (as the name suggests), the client will automatically be designated as the finance account.

If the checkbox is left unselected, a “Finance Account Details” section will appear at the bottom, allowing you to input the necessary finance account information separately.

Figure  - Finance Account Details

Signatory Field

Figure  - Signatory Field

Used on the first section of the fee proposal document when including the welcome letter. Here, we can select a user who will be the signatory for the welcome letter itself.

Proposal Letter Field

Figure  - Proposal Letter Field

As its name implies, used to enter the proposal letter that will be included in the fee proposal.

New Tabs Added

New tabs have also been added to the enquiry page, namely “Project Details” and “Schedule of Info”.

Project Details

Figure  - Project Details Tab

The “Project Details” section has been relocated to the project itself. These details are created as soon as an enquiry is first saved. If the enquiry was initially created from an existing project, you can associate it with that project by navigating to the “Enquiry Details” tab and selecting the relevant project from the “Project” dropdown.

If no project is selected in the dropdown, the system will automatically create a new project when the enquiry is saved, and the enquiry will be assigned to it.

This approach serves two key purposes:

Comprehensive Information Logging: It ensures that all relevant details, such as key dates, the principal designer, and other critical information, can be logged for each project.

Cross-Entity Transparency: It enables consistent access to the same information across all entity types—whether it is an enquiry, quote, or job—promoting transparency and seamless data sharing.

Schedule of Info

Figure  - Schedule of Info Tab

Used to store information (in the form of free text)  provided by the client (e.g. asbestos survey, architecture drawings, etc.). The information added here is then used in the last page of the fee proposal.

Functional Changes

Bulk Update for Enquiry Services

Figure  - Bulk Update for enquiry services.

Given an enquiry is multi-disciplinary, it can have many enquiry services set against it. If we wish to update a field for each of them, instead of having to go into each service to set the acceptance date for example, we can use the bulk update functionality to update multiple services at once.

Creating a Quote from an Enquiry

The process of transforming an enquiry to quote has been not changed – once all the compulsory fields have been completed, the quote can be created the usual way usin'
+ N'g the Task button located at the top.

Figure  - Button for creating a quote from an enquiry

Creating Jobs from an Enquiry

Once an enquiry is marked as “Accepted”, there is the option to create the jobs from the enquiry itself. Please, note that this will create a job for each quote associated with the quote that has been marked as “Accepted”.

Quote

Although the quotes look a lot like before, changes have also been made in terms of the fields and grids.

New Fields Added

Figure  - Exclusion and Limitations field.

The only new field is the “Exclusions and Limitations”. This field is used to enter things that are excluded from a project: for example, if someone is not qualified to perform a particular task, such as asbestos surveying, or even just listing things that will not be covered in the project itself.  The value entered here will be included in the fee proposal under the discipline linked to the quote.

Fields Made Read-Only

Figure  - Read-only fields on quotes

Several fields that were previously editable are now read-only to ensure a business-critical flow of data. For clarification, the different sections – where applicable – will have “(from Enquiry”) appended to it to signify that they were sourced from the enquiry.  These fields are purely for quick reference. A list of these fields can be found below:

Instruction (from Enquiry) Section

Job Type

Project

Property (UPRN)

Enquiry

Client Details (from Enquiry) Section

Client Account

Agent Details (from Enquiry) Section

Agent Account

Quote Details

Description of Work (from Enquiry)

Additional Fields Changes

Figure  - Organisation Unit Field

The only other field that has been changed is the “Organization Unit” field. This works in conjunction with the “Job Type” field, meaning that it will only auto-populate, if the job type has got an organisation unit set against it.

Grid Changes

Figure  - Quote Items Grid

Previously, we had the “Quote Sections” grid found at the bottom. Now, this has been replaced by the “Quote Items” grid which used to be found within a quote section itself.

Figure  - Adding a quote item.

Figure  - Product field within quote items

Within here, change has been made to the “Product” field: now, it will only show products that either do not have a job type assigned to them (e.g. general products that can be used by different disciplines) or the job type that is assigned to them matches the job type of the quote.

New Tabs Added

Like enquiries, additional tabs have been added.

